Solution :-

Prerequisites
Modular Arithmetic
Difference Arrays

Intuition
If the step size k i  was always 1, this would be a standard offline range update problem: you would use a multiplicative difference array to place a v 
i multiplier at l i , an inverse multiplier at r i +1, and then sweep the array once in O(N) time to calculate the final values.
eg: query [l = 0, r = 2, k = 1, v = 4]
Initial diff array: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
We multiply the start diff[0] by 4. To cancel the effect after the range, we multiply diff[3] by the modular inverse of 4 (let's denote this as 4 −1
, calculated via Fermat's Little Theorem).
diff becomes: [4, 1, 1, 4⁻¹, 1]
Propagate (Prefix Product): As we sweep left to right doing diff[i]=diff[i]∗diff[i−1], the array transforms into the actual multipliers:[4, 4, 4, 1, 1]

However, k i​
can be anything from 1 to N. Let's look at the two extreme ways to handle these jumps:
The Heavy (Brute Force), If k i​
is very large (e.g., k i =50,000 in an array of 100,000), 
a query touches like 2 elements. Running a simple for loop to jump through the array and update those elements takes no time, 
O( N ) per query. But if k i  is very small (e.g., k i =1), 
that same for loop touches 100,000 elements. Doing that for 100,000 queries will immediately result in TLE, O(N) per query.
The Light (Difference Array), we use a difference array that jumps by k i .
​
Placing the start and end values takes O(1) time per query. However, to resolve the array, we have to run an O(N) sweep to propagate the prefix products i.e., we have to 
run a separate O(N) sweep for every unique step size k. If the test cases contain Q unique k values, doing an O(N) sweep for each of them will also result in a TLE O(N∗Q).
We have two methods but with tradeoffs. The Brute Force is fast for large k but slow for small k. The difference array is fast for small k (because there are very few 
unique small numbers) but slow for large k. By drawing a line exactly at the square root of N say S, we get the best of both worlds:

If k≥S: We use the naive loop. The maximum number of jumps is  
S N = N N = N. 

Over Q queries, this takes at most O(Q N ) 
operations.
If k<S: We use the difference array. Even if every possible small k appears, there are only S unique step sizes. We group the queries by k, place all their values 
in O(1) time, and then run exactly S sweeps of size N. This takes at most O(N∗S) = O(N N ) operations.
By balancing the two methods, the worst-case scenario is strictly bounded to O((N+Q) N ) approx. 1e7 operations.

Complexity
Time Complexity: O(Q N +N N +Qlog(MOD)).
Heavy queries take up to O(Q N )
Modular inverses for light queries take O(Qlog(MOD))
Rebuilding the array takes O(N) for at most  N
​
unique small step sizes, totaling O(N N )
Space Complexity: O(N+Q)
to store the queries and a single N-sized diff array .

Code:-
class Solution {
    static final int MOD = 1000000007;

    long modpow(long a, long e) {
        long r = 1;
        a %= MOD;
        while (e > 0) {
            if ((e & 1) == 1) r = (r * a) % MOD;
            a = (a * a) % MOD;
            e >>= 1;
        }
        return r;
    }

    public int xorAfterQueries(int[] nums, int[][] queries) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int B = (int)Math.sqrt(n) + 1;

        // events[k][rem] = list of (position, multiplier)
        List<List<List<long[]>>> events = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i <= B; i++) {
            events.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        for (int k = 1; k <= B; k++) {
            List<List<long[]>> temp = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int rem = 0; rem < k; rem++) {
                temp.add(new ArrayList<>());
            }
            events.set(k, temp);
        }

        // Process queries
        for (int[] q : queries) {
            int l = q[0], r = q[1], k = q[2], v = q[3];

            if (k > B) {
                // direct update
                for (int i = l; i <= r; i += k) {
                    nums[i] = (int)((long)nums[i] * v % MOD);
                }
            } else {
                int rem = l % k;
                int start = (l - rem) / k;
                int end = (r - rem) / k;

                events.get(k).get(rem).add(new long[]{start, v});

                int maxT = (n - 1 - rem) / k;
                if (end + 1 <= maxT) {
                    long inv = modpow(v, MOD - 2);
                    events.get(k).get(rem).add(new long[]{end + 1, inv});
                }
            }
        }

        // Apply small k events
        for (int k = 1; k <= B; k++) {
            for (int rem = 0; rem < k; rem++) {
                List<long[]> ev = events.get(k).get(rem);
                if (ev.isEmpty()) continue;

                // sort events
                ev.sort(Comparator.comparingLong(a -> a[0]));

                // compress same positions
                List<long[]> comp = new ArrayList<>();
                for (long[] p : ev) {
                    if (!comp.isEmpty() && comp.get(comp.size() - 1)[0] == p[0]) {
                        long[] last = comp.get(comp.size() - 1);
                        last[1] = (last[1] * p[1]) % MOD;
                    } else {
                        comp.add(new long[]{p[0], p[1]});
                    }
                }

                // apply prefix multiplication
                long cur = 1;
                int ptr = 0;

                for (int t = 0, idx = rem; idx < n; t++, idx += k) {
                    while (ptr < comp.size() && comp.get(ptr)[0] == t) {
                        cur = (cur * comp.get(ptr)[1]) % MOD;
                        ptr++;
                    }
                    nums[idx] = (int)((long)nums[idx] * cur % MOD);
                }
            }
        }

        // Compute XOR
        int ans = 0;
        for (int x : nums) {
            ans ^= x;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
